# Stentor doesn't read environment variables for platform credentials --
# everything goes through `stentor auth <platform>`, which stores results
# in ~/.stentor/credentials.json (mode 0600). This file exists only to
# document, up front, exactly what each platform's `auth` step will ask
# for, so you can gather it before you start.

# --- Frictionless (no developer app needed) ---
# mastodon:  instance URL + a token from that instance's Settings -> Development
# bluesky:   handle + an app password from Settings -> App Passwords
# telegram:  a bot token from @BotFather + the chat id to post into
# discord:   a webhook URL from a channel you admin
# devto:     an API key from Settings -> Account -> DEV API Keys
# hashnode:  a personal access token from Account Settings -> Developer

# --- Self-serve app registration ---
# linkedin:  client id, client secret, redirect URI (LinkedIn Developer Portal,
#            app linked to a Page you admin, "Share on LinkedIn" product)
# reddit:    client id, client secret, redirect URI, your Reddit username,
#            a default subreddit -- NOTE: Reddit currently requires its own
#            manual "Responsible Builder Policy" approval before issuing new
#            app credentials at all (see README)
# twitter:   consumer key, consumer secret, access token, access token secret
#            (X Developer Portal, "Keys and tokens" tab)

# --- Platform review required ---
# facebook:  app id, app secret, redirect URI (Meta app, needs App Review for
#            pages_manage_posts to post to a real Page beyond your own test Pages)
# instagram: reuses facebook's credentials -- run `stentor auth facebook` first
# threads:   app id, app secret, redirect URI (separate Meta app product)
# tiktok:    client key, client secret, redirect URI (TikTok Developer app,
#            Content Posting API approval)
# youtube:   OAuth client id, client secret, redirect URI (Google Cloud Console) --
#            auth works, but posting does not: YouTube has no text-post API
